        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220">

                <pb facs="0528" n="432" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg432"/>

                <head>No. 199.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Welsh to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-07-11"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 11, 1879</date>.
                        (Received July 23.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 317.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your dispatch No. 296, of the
                    23d May last, directing that information be obtained for the Secretary of the
                    Treasury, respecting the amount of gold and silver coin and bullion and paper
                    currency in Great Britain, I have the honor to say that your inquiries were duly
                    transmitted to the British Government. Pending their reply, I requested a report
                    from Mr. George McHenry upon the same subject. I send herewith a copy of Mr.
                    McHenry’s letter to myself, and of the tables inclosed by it.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN WELSH.</signed></closer>




                <xi:include href="d220-a1.xml" xpointer="d220-a1"/>
